WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
In the Marvel Universe, the Eternal known&nbsp;
as Ikaris has been around for a very long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.960
time — hundreds of thousands of years, in&nbsp;
fact. But to many comic fans, he's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.280
something of a mystery. This is everything&nbsp;
you need to know about Ikaris from Eternals.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.160
Perhaps the most important thing about Ikaris&nbsp;
is that he isn't just any other Eternal. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.000
the so-called Prime Eternal. But&nbsp;
that hasn't always been the case.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.800
After the god-like Kronos' fateful experiments&nbsp;
with cosmic energy leaves his mind separated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.720
from his body, his two sons, Zuras and A'lars,&nbsp;
fight a conflict over the future of the Eternals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.000
When Zuras wins, A'lars leaves&nbsp;
for Jupiter's moon Titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.880
where he will later become father to Thanos. Then,&nbsp;
after the remaining Eternals form the collective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.080
Eternal consciousness known as the Uni-Mind,&nbsp;
it chooses Zuras to be the Prime Eternal.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.400
Zuras is later killed in battle with the&nbsp;
Celestials and Thena is selected as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.400
new Prime Eternal. In 1986's Eternals #9,&nbsp;
after Ikaris accuses Thena of treason over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.600
her loyalty to her Deviant lover, Kro, Thena&nbsp;
passes on the role of Prime Eternal to Ikaris.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.920
Being the Prime Eternal means more than getting&nbsp;
a fancy title, though. Among other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.080
whoever is named Prime Eternal is given the&nbsp;
privilege of initiating the formation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.720
the Uni-Mind — an act that has saved both the&nbsp;
Eternals and Earth on more than one occasion.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.440
Ikaris boasts a wide array of abilities,&nbsp;
including impressive super strength and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.560
durability. He expertly wields cosmic energy&nbsp;
that's often seen blasting from his eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.040
and he can manipulate this&nbsp;
ability in various ways.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.880
Unsurprisingly, these powers serve him&nbsp;
well in battle. In 1977's Eternals #14-#15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.960
Ikaris goes toe-to-toe with a cosmic-powered robot&nbsp;
designed to look, act, and display powers similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.160
to the Incredible Hulk. While not the real deal,&nbsp;
the cosmic-powered Hulk robot is no pushover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.200
And on top of that, Ikaris has faced all kinds&nbsp;
of similarly powerful challengers over the years.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.000
Similar to the other Eternals, Ikaris can fly.&nbsp;
And while they aren't his most used abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.200
he's also been shown to have both the capacity to&nbsp;
teleport and use some low-level psychic powers.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.200
If you're familiar with the world of ancient&nbsp;
myth, then you've probably already guessed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:15.120
the name Ikaris is inspired by Icarus, the boy&nbsp;
who flew too close to the sun. And in a mid-'80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.160
Marvel miniseries, the connection between&nbsp;
Ikaris and Icarus became much more literal.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.440
In Greco-Roman myth, Icarus is the son of&nbsp;
Daedalus, a genius forced by King Minos to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.400
design a labyrinth to contain the terrifying&nbsp;
Minotaur. When King Minos imprisons Daedalus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.600
and his family so that the secret of&nbsp;
the labyrinth can never be revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.760
Daedalus constructs his own flying&nbsp;
machines out of bird feathers and wax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.720
Upon their escape, Daedalus warns his son&nbsp;
not to fly too close to the sun, but Icarus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.080
ignores him, and when the wax of Icarus' flying&nbsp;
contraption melts, he plummets to his death.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.640
In 1986's Eternals #5, it's revealed that, in the&nbsp;
Marvel Universe, the Eternal Ikaris was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.400
the inspiration for this story. Centuries ago,&nbsp;
Ikaris had a son named Icarus. One day, believing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.600
his father to be imprisoned by Deviants, Icarus&nbsp;
attempted to use a mechanical flying device to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.200
find his father. Like the Icarus of myth, the boy&nbsp;
flew too high and eventually fell to his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.000
After learning of his son's fate, the Eternal&nbsp;
named himself Ikaris to honor his fallen child.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.280
One of the X-Men's most infamous villains is&nbsp;
Apocalypse, who first shows up using that name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.600
in 1986's X-Factor #5. It's since been revealed&nbsp;
that Apocalypse had faced a number of heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.000
before he met any of the X-Men, however.&nbsp;
For example, he fought a younger Thor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.400
2013's Uncanny Avengers #6, and at one point he&nbsp;
even faced off against Ikaris and the Eternals.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.760
In the 2000 one-shot New Eternals: Apocalypse&nbsp;
Now, the evil mutant unleashes his latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:49.040
sordid scheme. Ikaris and Apocalypse don't meet&nbsp;
face-to-face until toward the end of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.440
but when they do, it's suggested that they're&nbsp;
actually old enemies. The details are left a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.840
little murky, but Ikaris refers to the&nbsp;
villain as "En Sabah Nur," suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.160
their last encounter was centuries ago, when&nbsp;
Apocalypse was better known by that name.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.880
Ikaris also claims that he'd believed Apocalypse&nbsp;
had died in their last meeting. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.520
that might not seem like much of a reveal —&nbsp;
after all, as a popular Marvel Comics villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.920
Apocalypse is legally required to die&nbsp;
and be resurrected every few years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.960
But the fact that Ikaris and his&nbsp;
brethren so thoroughly defeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.200
Apocalypse as to believe him dead proves&nbsp;
just how formidable these heroes can be.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.600
"Who do you think's gonna lead the Avengers?"

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.560
"I could lead them."

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.000
Since the Eternals tend to live separate from&nbsp;
humanity, it's perhaps understandable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.360
some of them aren't exactly thrilled&nbsp;
to be sharing the Earth with humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.040
Consequently, some Eternals aren't quite&nbsp;
as dedicated to their safekeeping as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.360
they should be. Ikaris, however, has always&nbsp;
done everything he can to defend humankind.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.680
Ever since ancient times, Ikaris has gone above&nbsp;
and beyond to protect the people of Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.080
From his guidance of Noah's Ark to safety all&nbsp;
the way up to his allegiance with the Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.200
Ikaris has always advocated for the humans,&nbsp;
despite having advanced beyond them in many ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.480
This is why, when most of the Eternals&nbsp;
leave Earth after their battle with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.880
Inhuman villain Maelstrom, Ikaris is one&nbsp;
of the few selected by the Uni-Mind to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.440
behind — and continue to use his powers to&nbsp;
protect those who can't protect themselves.

00:05:16.156 --> 00:05:21.120
The idea of a superhero taking on a relatively&nbsp;
mundane job is nothing new. Spider-Man has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.240
a photographer, Daredevil has been a lawyer, and&nbsp;
the godlike Superman has enjoyed a thriving career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.440
as a reporter for the Daily Planet. Regardless,&nbsp;
it still feels like kind of a shock that the 1985&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.720
Eternals miniseries finds the noble and powerful&nbsp;
Ikaris working as a professional wrestler.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.840
In 1985's Eternals #1, it becomes apparent&nbsp;
that Ikaris has joined the ranks of Marvel's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.640
Unlimited Class Wrestling Federation as&nbsp;
Ike "The Iceberg" Harris. The issue even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.480
shows him in action in his wrestling digs,&nbsp;
and judging by the applause and cheers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.880
this is hardly Iceberg's first time in the&nbsp;
ring. Of course, it's eventually revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.240
Ikaris didn't join the UCWF because he enjoys&nbsp;
hitting people with folding chairs. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.200
Ikaris has been climbing the UCWF's ranks for&nbsp;
the chance to take on the wrestler Enigmo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.200
who's actually Cataphrax the Mighty,&nbsp;
a Deviant villain in disguise.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.560
The UCWF was one of the more prominent&nbsp;
Marvel fictional organizations in the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.000
Along with Ikaris, a number of other&nbsp;
heroes have joined and wrestled in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.760
UCWF — though the most prominent&nbsp;
was none other than Ben Grimm.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.000
Ikaris and the other Eternals claim the&nbsp;
distinction of starring in one of the few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.880
comic books that Neil Gaiman has written for&nbsp;
Marvel Comics. Comic book-wise, Gaiman is best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.760
known for his groundbreaking DC/Vertigo Sandman&nbsp;
series, though he's written a few projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:42.960
for Marvel, too. In 2003, he wrote 1602, which&nbsp;
introduced early 17th-century European versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.080
of most of Marvel's most popular heroes. Then,&nbsp;
after Marvel acquired the rights to Miracleman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.360
Gaiman wrote subsequent issues for that long-lost&nbsp;
character. And in 2006, he teamed up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.800
legendary artist John Romita Jr. to bring to&nbsp;
life another dormant property: the Eternals.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.480
The mid-aughts Eternals miniseries&nbsp;
begins with each of the godlike beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.800
stripped of their memories and living as&nbsp;
ordinary humans. The speedster Makkari,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.920
for example, is living as Mark Curry, a&nbsp;
med student. Sersi is a party organizer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:17.200
Thena is a scientist working for Stark Industries,&nbsp;
and Zuras is an alcoholic homeless man. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.520
eventually revealed that their memories have been&nbsp;
stripped by the mischievous Sprite, but before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.800
that becomes clear, it's Ikaris who sets out to&nbsp;
find the Eternals and reawaken their old memories.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.400
A few years later, the Eternals&nbsp;
fell to a horrible fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.880
and Ikaris was the last of them to&nbsp;
survive. When Avengers relaunched in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.080
it was led by a storyline involving the Dark&nbsp;
Celestials, a group of evil cosmic beings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.480
After the corpses of the true Celestials fall&nbsp;
to Earth, the Avengers set out on a quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.240
that ultimately reveals a dark secret about the&nbsp;
Eternals, humanity, and the Celestials themselves.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.640
When Iron Man travels to the Eternals' home of&nbsp;
Olympus, hoping to get some more information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.160
what has happened, he instead finds a massacre.&nbsp;
The only living Eternal he finds is Ikaris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.640
and even then the Prime Eternal doesn't&nbsp;
survive for long. Ikaris stays alive just long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.200
enough to reveal that his people erupted into&nbsp;
madness when they learned their true purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.680
Essentially, the Dark Celestials meant for&nbsp;
humanity to act as a virus against an army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.320
of insect-like monsters called the Horde, and&nbsp;
the Eternals were meant to cultivate that virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.360
and get humanity ready for the harvesting. Before&nbsp;
he succumbs to his wounds, Ikaris gives Iron Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:28.400
access to the Uni-Mind, which the Avengers are&nbsp;
later able to use to defeat the Dark Celestials.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.680
In 2021, the Eternals returned for the fifth&nbsp;
volume of their eponymous series. This story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.800
finds Ikaris and his immortal compatriots brought&nbsp;
back from the dead — they are eternal, after all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.680
Set loose in a battle against Thanos, Ikaris&nbsp;
is killed and rebirthed again, and only then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.480
discovers the horrible truth of the Eternals'&nbsp;
immortality: that they're resurrected at the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.680
of human lives. Unable to end this process without&nbsp;
endangering the Earth, the Eternals set out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.560
solve their problem... leading them into a fragile&nbsp;
alliance with their old enemies, the Deviants.

